<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Awa Province (Tokushima)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Awa was an old province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the east of the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>, corresponding to present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokushima-prefecture">Tokushima Prefecture</a>. In the 16th century it was the home base of the Miyoshi clan, retainers of the Hosokawa who rose to dominate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. It was overrun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-motochika">Chosokabe Motochika</a> in the 1580s and then given by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> to the Hachisuka family, who ruled it as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokushima">Tokushima</a> domain until 1871.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/awa-province-tokushima.jpg" alt="Awa Province (Tokushima)" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash_Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provinces_of_Japan-Awa_(Tokushima).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He overran <b>Awa</b>, Sanuki and Iyo and by 1585 controlled most of Shikoku.</blockquote><a href="/state/chosokabe-clan-domain">Chosokabe clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>The Miyoshi were retainers of the Hosokawa deputy shoguns, with a base in <b>Awa</b> on Shikoku.</blockquote><a href="/state/miyoshi-clan-domain">Miyoshi clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>After Toyotomi Hideyoshi conquered Shikoku in 1585, he granted <b>Awa</b> province to his retainer Hachisuka Iemasa, who built Tokushima castle.</blockquote><a href="/state/tokushima-domain">Tokushima Domain</a></li></ul></section>
